# Measured 2026-09-16 against Xvfb 2:21.1.22-1ubuntu1 on :95 from a raw socket
# (scripts/xw11-probe-bigreq.py). Xwayland was NOT measured.
# Same shape as badlength-bigreq-short.hex -- setup, QueryExtension, Enable,
# then the probe as request 3 -- but line 1 is a big-form GetProperty claiming
# 4194304 words, one word OVER the 4194303 that connection's own BigReqEnable
# reply had just advertised (16777212 bytes, recon/wire.md 2; the same ceiling
# both servers gave in 2026-09 and the same one Xvfb gave again today). That is
# block (d) of the probe's output.
# Line 2 is the whole answer: one 32-byte BadLength naming seq=3, major=20 --
# byte for byte the answer to the too-SHORT form, and the connection stays open
# for both. 0x0FFFFFFF words (a gigabyte) -- block (e), the next one in the same
# run -- answered the same thirty-two bytes, so the server does not care how far
# over the ceiling it is.
# The four NoOperations and the GetInputFocus behind the probe drew nothing, so
# here too the server ate four bytes and re-framed the stream from the length
# word. The thing being pinned is the REFUSAL: a server never buffers the
# gigabyte, and neither may the proxy (xw11/wire.py's ceiling).
1400000000004000
0010030000000000000014000000000000000000000000000000000000000000
